Q: &lt;img&gt; attribute in the first element is different than other elements, preventing me from scraping its URL A: <p>Simply select all of the images, iterate over the <code>ResultSet</code> and check if an attribute is available to extract its value and print it or append it do a <code>list</code> or  <code>set</code> in case of avoiding duplicates.</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>from bs4 import BeautifulSoup

html = '''
&lt;img class=&quot;class_name&quot; src=&quot;https://website1.png&quot;/&gt;
&lt;img class=&quot;class_name late&quot; data-src=&quot;https://website2.png&quot;/&gt;
&lt;img class=&quot;class_name late&quot; data-src=&quot;https://website3.png&quot;/&gt;
'''
soup = BeautifulSoup(html)

for link in soup.select('img.class_name'):
    if link.get('src'):
        print(link.get('src'))
    else:
        print(link.get('data-src'))
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>https://website1.png
https://website2.png
https://website3.png
</code></pre>

Q: Problem displaying png file from web server to client web browser in C A: <ol>
<li><p><code>send(new_socket, http_header, sizeof(http_header), 0);</code> should be <code>strlen(http_header)</code> instead of <code>sizeof</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>if (file &gt; 0) {</code> doesn't do anything, you already check if the fil exist after you open it, and as it's a pointer you only want to check if it's NULL or not.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>fread()</code> returns a size_t not a long (they may be the same type of course).</p>
</li>
<li><p>You have use bytes_read instead of <code>sizeof(dbuffer)</code> in <code>send()</code> to a short read results in a short write (but see refinement for partial writes which are normal).</p>
</li>
<li><p>(not fixed) Browsers may request other files.  Firefox, for instance, asks for a <code>/favicon.ico</code> so you want to reject that with a 403.  With the above changes curl returns file as is.</p>
</li>
<li><p>As you run that send in a <code>while(1)</code> you probably want to move the <code>rewind(file)</code> into that <code>while</code>-loop and you may need to <code>clearerr(file)</code> too.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>        size_t bytes_read;
        do {
            bytes_read = fread(dbuffer, 1, BUFFER_LEN, file);
            ssize_t bytes_sent;
            for(size_t n = 0; n &lt; bytes_read; n += bytes_sent) {
                bytes_sent = send(new_socket, dbuffer + n, bytes_read - n, 0);
                if(bytes_sent == -1) {
                    perror(&quot;send failed&quot;);
                    goto err;
                }
            }
        // if feof(file) then we just finished reading the file,
        // otherwise we may want to perror() something.
        } while(bytes_read == BUFFER_LEN);
err:
        close(new_socket);
        clearerr(file);
        rewind(file);
</code></pre>

Q: string literals and constexpr string msg = format(...) with GCC C++23 A: <p>You can use <code>std::to_chars</code> which is <code>constexpr</code> since C++23 (<a href="https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/utility/to_chars" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/utility/to_chars</a>). But it won't be enough. Compile-time concatenation is not straightforward.</p>
<p>It cannot be achieved from <code>std::string_view</code> for reasons explained there: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/73478427/concatenating-string-view-objects">Concatenating string_view objects</a>.</p>
<p>It does not always work with <code>std::string</code> because it dynamically allocates its data. Thus it can be only used if the string is destroyed at the end of the <code>constexpr</code> expression. It can accidentally work if Short String Optimization applies but it is implementation and data dependant.</p>
<p>So the issue is a bit more complex than it seems.</p>
<p>First of all, I will propose a user-defined compile-time string literal, with well defined concatenation.</p>
<p>This can be done like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;string_view&gt;

// LiteralString is a literal class type, usable as constant template parameter
// N does not includes null final character
template &lt;std::size_t N&gt;
struct LiteralString {
    consteval LiteralString(const char* s) { std::copy(s, s + N, &amp;data[0]); }
    consteval operator std::string_view() const { return {data, N}; }

    static constexpr std::size_t size = N;
    char data[N]{};
};

// deduction guide for string literals
template &lt;std::size_t N&gt;
LiteralString(const char (&amp;s)[N]) -&gt; LiteralString&lt;N - 1&gt;;

// user-define string litteral
template &lt;LiteralString LS&gt;
constexpr auto operator&quot;&quot;_ls() {
    return LS;
}

// compile-time only concatenation
template &lt;std::size_t Ln, std::size_t Rn&gt;
consteval auto operator+(LiteralString&lt;Ln&gt; const&amp; lhs,
                         LiteralString&lt;Rn&gt; const&amp; rhs) {
    constexpr std::size_t NewSize = Ln + Rn;
    char data[NewSize]{};
    auto ptr = std::copy(lhs.data, lhs.data + Ln, &amp;data[0]);
    std::copy(rhs.data, rhs.data + Rn, ptr);
    return LiteralString&lt;NewSize&gt;{data};
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that I will need to construct from <code>const char* s</code> later, which does not allow to deduce <code>N</code>. If I want to initialize from a simple string literal such as <code>&quot;Hello&quot;</code> I will have to deduce <code>N</code> from the string length and I achieve that through a deduction guide.</p>
<p>Then lets work on the creation from a number, using <code>std::to_chars</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>namespace Details {
// number of digits of i in base 10
template &lt;unsigned int i&gt;
consteval std::size_t NbDigits() {
    if constexpr (i &lt;= 9) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return NbDigits&lt;i / 10&gt;() + 1;
    }
}
}  // namespace Details

// compile-time creation of a string literal from an integer
template &lt;unsigned int i&gt;
consteval auto to_string() {
    // getting the needed size
    static constexpr std::size_t buf_size = Details::NbDigits&lt;i&gt;() + 1;
    char buf[buf_size]{};
    // using to_chars constexpr-ness
    *(std::to_chars(buf, buf + buf_size, i).ptr) = 0;
    return LiteralString&lt;buf_size&gt;(buf);
}
</code></pre>
<p>It's quite straightforward. I just need to be &quot;smart&quot; about the buffer size. Note that a bit more work would be needed to handle other types than <code>unsigned int</code> (handling signedness, representation,...).</p>
<p>Now we can play with it:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int main() {
    static constexpr auto full_message =
        &quot;The number is &quot;_ls + to_string&lt;42&gt;() + &quot;.&quot;_ls;
    // casting to string_view for display, no need to overload operator &lt;&lt;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::string_view{full_message};
}
</code></pre>
<p>Outputs:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The number is 42.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/TEnKf1dYx" rel="nofollow noreferrer">LIVE</a></p>
<p>Final note: as a bonus, a <code>LiteralString</code> can be used as a template parameter, if needed.</p>

Q: Document a Vue3 Generic Component such that typedoc can list, $emits, $props and $slots A: <p>Until now I was able to create the following vue3 type definition helper.</p>
<ul>
<li>It handles both exposing types to tools like typedoc resulting in human readable documentation, as well as keep the types usable during development.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>import type { ComponentPublicInstance, SlotsType, EmitsOptions } from &quot;vue&quot;;

/**
 * Converts a tuple-format emits type `{ eventName: [arg1: T1, ...] }` to
 * the function-value format `{ eventName: (arg1: T1, ...) =&gt; void }` required
 * by `EmitsOptions`. Use this when passing a tuple-format emits type to
 * `TGenericVue3Component` or `TGenericVue3ComponentConstructor`.
 * @group Types
 * @category Types
 */
export type TTupleEmitsToOptions&lt;T extends Record&lt;string, any[]&gt;&gt; = {
    [K in keyof T]: (...args: T[K]) =&gt; void;
};

/**
 * Helper type for defining the return type of a Generic Vue Component constructor.
 * Ensures TypeDoc picks up properties (via Readonly&lt;P&gt;) and Vue integration (via ComponentPublicInstance).
 * 
 * Usage:
 * export default {} as unknown as new &lt;T&gt;(props: IProps&lt;T&gt;) =&gt; TGVue3GenericComponent&lt;IProps&lt;T&gt;, IEmits&lt;T&gt;, ISlots&lt;T&gt;, IExposed&gt;;
 * @group Types
 * @category Types
 */
export type TGenericVue3Component&lt;TProps, TEmits extends EmitsOptions = {}, TSlots extends Record&lt;string, any&gt; = {}, TExposed extends Record&lt;string, any&gt; = {}&gt;
    = ComponentPublicInstance&lt;TProps, {}, {}, {}, {}, TEmits, TProps, {}, false, any, {}, SlotsType&lt;TSlots&gt;&gt; &amp; TExposed &amp; Readonly&lt;TProps&gt; &amp; {
    $props: TProps;
    $slots: TSlots;
    $emits: TEmits;
};

/**
 * Helper type for defining the return type of a Generic Vue Component constructor.
 * Ensures TypeDoc picks up properties (via Readonly&lt;P&gt;) and Vue integration (via ComponentPublicInstance).
 * 
 * Note: This definition does not work well will generics. In case your interfaces need generic use @see { @link TGenericVue3Component }
 * 
 * Usage:
 * export default {} as unknown as TGVue3GenericComponentConstructor&lt;IProps, IEmits, ISlots, IExposed&gt;;
 * @group Types
 * @category Types
 */
export type TGenericVue3ComponentConstructor&lt;TProps, TEmits extends EmitsOptions = {}, TSlots extends Record&lt;string, any&gt; = {}, TExposed extends Record&lt;string, any&gt; = {}&gt;
    = new (props: TProps) =&gt; TGenericVue3Component&lt;typeof props, TEmits, TSlots, TExposed&gt;;

</code></pre>
<h2>Usage</h2>
<p>You have to cast the <code>export default {}</code> to <code>unknown</code> first, then you cast it again to a constructor definition which outputs TGenericVue3Component with your props-, emits- and slots-types/interfaces. In my case:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>IGVue3DropdownProps&lt;TId, TData&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>TGVue3DropdownEmits&lt;TId, TData&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>IGVue3DropdownSlots&lt;TId, TData&gt;</code></li>
<li><code>IGVue3DropdownExposed</code></li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script lang=&quot;ts&quot;&gt;
/* We will use this script-block to export type definitions and documentation. */
import type { IDropdownItem, IGVue3DropdownExposed, IGVue3DropdownProps, IGVue3DropdownRefreshOptions, IGVue3DropdownSlots, TGVue3DropdownEmits } from &quot;./GVue3Dropdown.types&quot;;

/**
 * ...
 * @class
 */
export default {} as unknown as new &lt;TId extends TListEntryPropertyName, TData&gt;(props: IGVue3DropdownProps&lt;TId, TData&gt;)
=&gt; TGenericVue3Component&lt;
    typeof props,
    TTupleEmitsToOptions&lt;TGVue3DropdownEmits&lt;TId, TData&gt;&gt;, /* We need to wrap our emits type in `TTupleEmitsToOptions` for the vue compiler to be happy. */
    IGVue3DropdownSlots&lt;TId, TData&gt;,
    IGVue3DropdownExposed
&gt;;

&lt;/script&gt;


&lt;script setup lang=&quot;ts&quot; generic=&quot;TId extends TListEntryPropertyName, TData&quot;&gt;
/* And this script-setup-block to implement the actual component. */

...

/* Using `defineProps` with our props interface. */
const props = defineProps&lt;IGVue3DropdownProps&lt;TId, TData&gt;&gt;();

/* When actually defining emits we use our type `TGVue3DropdownEmits` directly. */
const emit = defineEmits&lt;TGVue3DropdownEmits&lt;TId, TData&gt;&gt;();

/* Slots can be simply defined without passing any types or interfaces. */
const slots = useSlots();

/* API will be exposed in 2 steps. */
const exposedApi: IGVue3DropdownExposed = {
    refresh: refreshFromProps
};
defineExpose(exposedApi);


&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<h2>How it displays</h2>
<p>This is nicely picked up by typedoc like:
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/IYrBAjoW.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/IYrBAjoW.png" alt="Typedoc nicely formatted properties etc." /></a></p>
<p>and VS-Code typehints
<img src="https://i.sstatic.net/5BbUIbHO.png" alt="VS-Code typehints on hover" /></p>
<hr />
<p>For reference these are the defined types:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>import type { IListEntry, ListTextFilter, TListEntryPropertyName } from &quot;@gsag/listengine&quot;;

/**
 * @category Comp / ListEngine / Dropdown
 * @module GVue3Dropdown.types
 */

/**
 * The `v-model` payload for `GVue3Dropdown`.
 * Use a one-element array for single-select mode and an array of keys for multi-select mode.
 * Empty selection is represented by `[]`, `null`, or `undefined`.
 * @category Comp / ListEngine / Dropdown
 */
export type TGVue3DropdownModelValue&lt;TId extends TListEntryPropertyName&gt; = TId[] | null | undefined;

/**
 * Dynamic fetch callback for `GVue3Dropdown`.
 * @category Comp / ListEngine / Dropdown
 */
export type TDropdownFetchCallback&lt;TId extends TListEntryPropertyName, TData&gt; = (listTextFilter: ListTextFilter&lt;TId, IDropdownItem&lt;TId, TData&gt;&gt;) =&gt; Promise&lt;Array&lt;IDropdownItem&lt;TId, TData&gt;&gt;&gt;;

/**
 * Interface for dropdown items.
 * @category Comp / ListEngine / Dropdown
 */
export interface IDropdownItem&lt;TId extends TListEntryPropertyName, TData&gt;
{
    /** Unique identifier for the item. Supports `string` and `number`. */
    key: TId;
    /** The actual value/payload of the item. */
    value: TData;
}

/**
 * Internationalization options for GVue3Dropdown.
 * @category Comp / ListEngine / Dropdown
 */
export interface I18nGVue3Dropdown
{
    /** i18n: User call to action to search or chose options */
    searchOrSelectCallToAction?: string;
    /** i18n: There are no options at all. */
    noOptionsAvailable?: string;
    /** i18n: There are no options for the search.  */
    noOptionsFound?: string;
    /** i18n: Search is currently running. */
    searching?: string;
}

/**
 * Options for the exposed `refresh()` method.
 * @category Comp / ListEngine / Dropdown
 */
export interface IGVue3DropdownRefreshOptions
{
    /**
     * Resets transient UI state such as the open state and current search text before refreshing.
     * Default: `true`
     */
    resetUi?: boolean;

    /**
     * Clears the current filter before refreshing and rehydrates the selection from the current `modelValue`.
     * Default: `true`
     */
    resetFilter?: boolean;
}

/**
 * Public instance API exposed by `GVue3Dropdown`.
 * @category Comp / ListEngine / Dropdown
 */
export interface IGVue3DropdownExposed
{
    /**
     * Refreshes the dropdown from the current props and `modelValue`.
     * Useful when the component stays mounted across sessions and needs to rehydrate its internal selection state.
     */
    refresh(options?: IGVue3DropdownRefreshOptions): Promise&lt;void&gt;;
}

/**
 * Slots definition for GVue3Dropdown
 * @category Comp / ListEngine / Dropdown
 */
export interface IGVue3DropdownSlots&lt;TId extends TListEntryPropertyName, TData&gt;
{
    /**
     * Slot for rendering a single option in the dropdown list.
     * @param props The list entry containing the option item.
     */
    option?(props: { listEntry: IListEntry&lt;TId, IDropdownItem&lt;TId, TData&gt;&gt;; }): any;

    /**
     * Slot for rendering one selected multi-select entry.
     * Receives the selected list entry and a toggle callback for deselection.
     */
    &quot;option-selected&quot;?(props: { listEntry: IListEntry&lt;TId, IDropdownItem&lt;TId, TData&gt;&gt;; onToggleSelection: () =&gt; void; }): any;

    /**
     * Slot for rendering the create-action row shown as the last dropdown entry.
     * Receives the current typed search text and a trigger callback.
     */
    &quot;option-create&quot;?(props: { searchText: string; onCreate: () =&gt; void; }): any;
}

/**
 * Emits definition for GVue3Dropdown.
 * `update:modelValue` always emits an array-shaped selection payload.
 * @category Comp / ListEngine / Dropdown
 */
export type TGVue3DropdownEmits&lt;TId extends TListEntryPropertyName, TData&gt; = {
    &quot;update:modelValue&quot;: [value: TGVue3DropdownModelValue&lt;TId&gt;];
    &quot;add-selection&quot;: [item: IDropdownItem&lt;TId, TData&gt;];
    &quot;remove-selection&quot;: [item: IDropdownItem&lt;TId, TData&gt;];
    &quot;action-create&quot;: [searchText: string];
};

/**
 * @deprecated Use `TGVue3DropdownEmits` instead.
 * @category Comp / ListEngine / Dropdown
 */
export type IGVue3DropdownEmits&lt;TId extends TListEntryPropertyName, TData&gt; = TGVue3DropdownEmits&lt;TId, TData&gt;;

/**
 * @category Comp / ListEngine / Dropdown
 */
export interface IGVue3DropdownProps&lt;TId extends TListEntryPropertyName, TData&gt;
{
    /**
     * A list of options.
     * Cannot be used together with fetch.
     * When set it is recommended to also set @see { @link IGVue3DropdownProps.search }.
     */
    options?: Array&lt;IDropdownItem&lt;TId, TData&gt;&gt;;

    /**
     * Dynamic fetch callback.
     * Cannot be used together with `options`.
     * @returns A promise resolving to a list of dropdown items.
     */
    fetch?: TDropdownFetchCallback&lt;TId, TData&gt;;

    /**
     * When using an async fetch it is a good idea to defer the fetching process to prevent excessive API calls.
     * The minimum value will be `10` at all times. There is no upper limit.
     */
    deferFetchByMilliseconds?: number;

    /** Whether multiple items can be selected. Default: false */
    enableMultiSelect?: boolean;

    /** Enables the optional create-action row for the current search text. Default: false */
    enableCreateAction?: boolean;

    /**
     * Callback to convert a value to a string for display in the input (if single select) or bubbles.
     * Default: String(value)
     */
    display?: (value: IDropdownItem&lt;TId, TData&gt;) =&gt; string;

    /**
     * Callback used to convert a value to searchable text.
     */
    search?: (value: IDropdownItem&lt;TId, TData&gt;) =&gt; string;

    /**
     * `v-model` binding for the selected key or keys.
     * Use a one-element array for single-select mode and an array of keys for multi-select mode.
     * Empty selection is represented by `[]`, `null`, or `undefined`.
     */
    modelValue?: TGVue3DropdownModelValue&lt;TId&gt;;

    /**
     * Additional class for the dropdown container.
     */
    containerClass?: string | Record&lt;string, boolean&gt;;

    i18n?: I18nGVue3Dropdown;
}

</code></pre>
<p>Disclaimer: I used all tools available to me:</p>
<ul>
<li>Official Typescript and Vue3 documentations</li>
<li>Gemini 3 (Pro), ChatGPT as well as Claude Sonnet 4.6</li>
</ul>
<p>Tested in production.</p>

Q: How to remove the top (or navigation) toolbar background tint in iOS 27 A: <p>This problem is a bit difficult to reproduce, most arrangements do not show a material background behind the toolbar. However, I found that it did happen if <code>.scrollEdgeEffectStyle(.hard, for: .top)</code> is applied to the <code>ScrollView</code>.</p>
<p>To fix,</p>
<ul>
<li><em>either</em> change the edge effect style from <code>.hard</code> to <code>.soft</code></li>
<li><em>or</em>, if you don't want a fade effect either, apply <code>.scrollEdgeEffectHidden(for: .top)</code> instead.</li>
</ul>
<p>Here is a little example to show it working:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>struct ContentView: View {
    let loremIpsum = &quot;Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit, sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat. Duis aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse cillum dolore eu fugiat nulla pariatur. Excepteur sint occaecat cupidatat non proident, sunt in culpa qui officia deserunt mollit anim id est laborum.&quot;

    var body: some View {
        TabView {
            Tab(&quot;Recipies&quot;, systemImage: &quot;fork.knife&quot;) {
                NavigationStack {
                    NavigationLink(&quot;Show a good recipie&quot;) {
                        nestedContent
                    }
                }
            }
            Tab(&quot;Settings&quot;, systemImage: &quot;gearshape&quot;) {
                Text(&quot;Settings&quot;)
            }
        }
    }

    private var nestedContent: some View {
        GeometryReader { geo in
            ScrollView {
                VStack {
                    headerImage(topInset: geo.safeAreaInsets.top)
                    Text(loremIpsum)
                        .font(.title3)
                        .padding()
                        .frame(height: 1000, alignment: .top)
                }
            }
            .ignoresSafeArea()
            // .scrollEdgeEffectStyle(.automatic, for: .top) // 👈 causes background effect
            .scrollEdgeEffectHidden(for: .top) // 👈 prevents it
            .toolbar {
                ToolbarItem(placement: .topBarTrailing) {
                    Button(&quot;Options&quot;, systemImage: &quot;ellipsis&quot;) {}
                }
            }
        }
    }

    private func headerImage(topInset: CGFloat) -&gt; some View {
        Image(systemName: &quot;fish.fill&quot;)
            .resizable()
            .scaledToFit()
            .padding(.top, topInset)
            .padding([.leading, .trailing, .bottom])
            .foregroundStyle(.orange.gradient)
            .background(.mint.opacity(0.3))
    }
}
</code></pre>
